~C. The paragraph has stuff in it.#Incorrect. This is not precise. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The author used words to make words.#Incorrect. This does not explain craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U01_L03_Q09::[MLA.CW.WR.07] Passage: "The room was small" and "The room was cramped." How does "cramped" differ from "small"?{
~A. It means the room is larger.#Incorrect. It still suggests small space. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. It has the same denotation but a more uncomfortable connotation.#Correct. "Cramped" suggests discomfort while still meaning limited space.
~C. It removes all tone.#Incorrect. It adds tone. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It makes the room sound formal.#Incorrect. The effect is discomfort, not formality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U01_L03_Q10::[MLA.CW.COM.03] Passage: "The trophy glared from the shelf." What tone is suggested by "glared"?{
